P={
'011':'Model plan: draw a leaf on one side with sunlight, water and carbon dioxide going in and glucose and oxygen coming out (photosynthesis, in chloroplasts, uses light). On the other side draw an animal or plant cell with glucose and oxygen going in and carbon dioxide, water and energy coming out (respiration, in mitochondria, all day). Write both word equations and give the poster a title, labels and colours. ## Project: a poster comparing the two processes; one possible plan.',
'018':'Model plan: take a plastic bottle, balloons, straws and rubber bands for a respiratory model (bottle = chest, balloon inside = lung, balloon at the bottom = diaphragm), or red and blue straws/tubes and a pump bulb for a circulatory model (heart, arteries, veins, capillaries). Label every part and write one sentence on its job. ## Project: a model from recycled material; one possible plan.',
'024':'Model plan: choose a disease such as dengue, malaria or hepatitis; make posters and a short speech about its cause, signs and prevention (e.g. cover water tanks and drain standing water; wash hands; vaccinate); present them in your school and neighbourhood and record how many people you reached. ## Project: an awareness campaign; one possible plan.',
'031':'Model plan: a two-column poster. Physical change: no new substance, usually reversible (melting ice, dissolving sugar, cutting paper). Chemical change: new substance, usually irreversible, with signs such as gas bubbles, colour change, light or heat (burning wood, rusting iron, baking a cake, milk turning sour). Add drawings. ## Project: a comparison poster; one possible plan.',
'036':'Model plan: for example carbon (6 protons, 6 neutrons, 6 electrons; shells 2, 4): a ball of clay or foam for the nucleus with 6 red (proton) and 6 blue (neutron) beads, and wire rings holding 2 and 4 electron beads, mounted on a stand. Label protons, neutrons, electrons and shells. ## Project: a free-standing atom model; one possible plan.',
'041':'Model plan: lithium atom: nucleus of 3 protons and 4 neutrons, shells with 2 and 1 electrons. Lithium ion Li+: the one outer electron is removed, so the ion has 2 electrons in one shell and a +1 charge. Show both with play dough balls on chart paper and write: the ion has one electron less and a positive charge. ## Project: two models and the difference; one possible plan.',
'050':'Model plan: filter oily water with layers of sand, a cheese cloth and a spoon to skim the oil; observe how clear the water becomes. Make a list of solutions and their uses, e.g. salt water (cooking), sugar syrup (sweet drinks), ORS (treating dehydration), brass (utensils), air (breathing). ## Project: a practical filtration activity and a list; one possible plan.',
'058':'Model plan: a plastic bottle one third full of water, a cork with a valve and a pump; pumping air in raises the pressure and the water is pushed out downwards, so the rocket moves up (Newton\'s third law: action and reaction pair). Do it outdoors, away from people. ## Project: a water rocket; one possible plan.',
'068':'Model plan: nail small nails in a row at different distances on a card or board, stretch rubber bands between them (shorter, tighter bands give higher pitch; longer or looser ones give lower pitch), pluck them and write the notes from low to high. ## Project: a rubber-band instrument showing pitch and frequency; one possible plan.',
'077':'Model plan: a box inside a bigger box with the gap filled with insulating material (thermocol, cotton or newspaper), a tight lid, and silver foil on the inside to reflect heat; put ice inside and check how long it takes to melt compared with an uninsulated container. ## Project: an insulated cooler; one possible plan.',
'084':'Model plan: record for five days the litres used for drinking, washing, brushing, bathing, cooking and cleaning (direct use), add the virtual water in food and goods (e.g. 1 kg of wheat about 1500 litres), find the daily average and compare with classmates; suggest saving water by closing taps, fixing leaks and reusing water. ## Project: calculate your water footprint; results depend on your own data, so no single value is given (Not verified).',
'091':'Model plan: a lamp for the Sun in the centre, a globe tilted 23.5 degrees on a stand moved around it, keeping the tilt pointing the same way; when the north end leans towards the lamp it is summer there and winter in the south, and the reverse six months later; mark equinoxes and solstices. ## Project: a model for the seasons; one possible plan.'}
for pg,t in P.items():
    f=D+f'p{pg}.txt'; L=open(f,encoding='utf8').read().split('\n')
    i=next(k for k,l in enumerate(L) if l.startswith('q ') and 'Project' in l and 'project' not in l.lower().replace('project work','') or (l.startswith('q ') and 'Project work' in l))
    j=next(k for k in range(i,len(L)) if L[k].startswith('endexercise'))
    if L[j-1].startswith('sol'): continue
    L.insert(j,'sol '+t)
    open(f,'w',encoding='utf8',newline='\n').write('\n'.join(L))
